# Copy this file into the ssbx profile folder of Colima, next to the config
# (~/.config/colima/ssbx/hosts on Linux, ~/.colima/ssbx/hosts on macOS), and
# fill in your tokens. After editing it, run `ssbx reload`.
#
# Each line is:   host   kind   token
#
# The sandbox may only read from these hosts: git clone, git fetch, GET
# requests and Jira searches. It cannot push, open merge requests, comment,
# run pipelines or change anything else. The proxy adds the token to the
# requests it lets through; the sandbox never sees the token.
#
# Still give it read-only tokens. Then even a mistake in the proxy cannot
# turn into a write.
#
# kind is one of:
#   gitlab      GitLab token (read_api, read_repository)
#   github      GitHub fine-grained token (Contents: read, Metadata: read)
#   atlassian   Jira or Confluence Cloud: your-email:api-token
#   bearer      anything that takes "Authorization: Bearer <token>",
#               for example Jira or Confluence Data Center

# gitlab.com               gitlab      glpat-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# gitlab.example.com       gitlab      glpat-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# github.com               github      github_pat_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# api.github.com           github      github_pat_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# example.atlassian.net    atlassian   you@example.com:ATATT3xxxxxxxxxxxx
# jira.example.com         bearer      xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
